"Newport Bay Draft Time Schedule Order R8-2025-0003, Amendment To Time Schedule Order R8-2019-0050, As Amended by Order R8-2023-0063 (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2025/r8-2025-0003.pdf)\nciteturn1082view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view1\",\"id\":82}); Total lines: 541\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTentative Resolution R8-2026-0014 (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2026/r8-2026-0014.pdf)\nciteturn1082view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view0\",\"id\":87}); Total lines: 267\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nR8-2020-0006 Regional De Minimis Permit (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2020/R8-2020-0006.pdf)\nciteturn1082view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view2\",\"pattern\":\"continued\"}); Total lines: 1765\nL121@P3: issues a discharge authorization letter, which shall include a self-monitoring program for\nL122@P3: the proposed discharge.\nL123@P3: D. Termination of Coverage\nL124@P3: The Discharger shall inform the Regional Water Board by a letter if coverage under this\nL125@P3: Order is no longer needed. Upon receipt of said letter, the Regional Water Board\nL126@P3: Executive Officer or a designee shall issue a letter terminating coverage under this\nL127@P3: Order.\nL128@P3: III. FINDINGS\nL129@P3: The Regional Water Board finds:\nL130@P3: A. Background.\nL131@P3: Order No. R8-2015-0004, NPDES No. CAG998001, was adopted by the California\nL132@P3: Regional Water Quality Control Board, Santa Ana Region (hereinafter Regional Water\nL133@P3: Board), on June 19, 2015, for discharges to surface waters of various types of wastes\nL134@P3-4: De Minimis OrderOrder No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL135@P4: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to Surface Waters Page 5 of 12\nL136@P4: that pose an insignificant threat to water quality. Order No. R8-2015-0004 will expire on\nL137@P4: June 30, 2020.\nL138@P4: Sixty-three Dischargers have been authorized to discharge wastewater under Order No.\nL139@P4: R8-2015-0004; of these, 44 are still active. It is anticipated that many of these existing\nL140@P4: Dischargers will be submitting renewal applications for continued discharges. It is also\nL141@P4: anticipated that many more Dischargers will wish to obtain permit coverage for the types\nL142@P4: of discharges regulated under this general order. The demand for permit issuance will\nL143@P4: far exceed the available staff resources to develop and bring individual tentative waste\nL144@P4: discharge requirements to the Board for adoption. These circumstances necessitate\nL145@P4: the renewal of these general waste discharge requirements.\nL146@P4: For the purposes of this Order, references to the “discharger” or “permittee” in\nL147@P4: applicable federal and State laws, regulations, plans, or policy are held to be equivalent\nL148@P4: to references to the Discharger herein.\nL149@P4: B. Discharge Description. This Order regulates discharges (as listed in Section I.\nL150@P4: Discharge Information, above) to surface waters that pose an insignificant threat to\nL151@P4: water quality. The discharges are to surface waters, including estuarine and ocean\nL152@P4: waters, within the Santa Ana Region.\nL153@P4: C. Legal Authorities. This Order is issued pursuant to Chapter 5.5, Division 7 of the\nL154@P4: California Water Code (Section 13370 et seq.) and Section 402 of the federal Clean\nL155@P4: Water Act (CWA) and implementing regulations adopted by the U.S. Environmental\nL156@P4: Protection Agency (USEPA). This Order serves as Waste Discharge Requirements\nL157@P4: (WDRs) pursuant to Article 4, Chapter 4 of the CWC (Section 13260 et seq.). It shall\nL158@P4: also serve as a National Pollutant Discharge Elimination System (NPDES) permit for\nL159@P4: point source discharges to surface waters.\nL160@P4: D. Background and Rationale for Requirements. The Regional Water Boarddeveloped\nL161@P4: the requirements in this Order based on information obtained through issuance and\nL162@P4: enforcement of the prior general permits for groundwater cleanup discharges, through\nL163@P4: monitoring and reporting programs, and other available information. The Fact Sheet\nL164@P4: (Attachment F), which contains background information and rationale for Order\nL165@P4: requirements, is hereby incorporated into this Order and, thus constitutes part of the\nL166@P4: Findings for this Order. Attachments A through F are also incorporated into thisOrder.\nL167@P4-5: De Minimis OrderOrder No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL168@P5: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to Surface Waters Page 6 of 12\nL169@P5: E. California Environmental Quality Act (CEQA). Under Water Code Section 13389,this\nL170@P5: action to adopt waste discharge requirements that also serve as an NPDES permit is\nL171@P5: exempt from the provisions of CEQA, Public Resources Code Section 21000 et seq.\nL1242@P37: or subsections of this Order not specifically identified as “not applicable” are fully applicable to\nL1243@P37: the Dischargers authorized to discharge wastewater under this Order.\nL1244@P37: I. BACKGROUND\nL1245@P37: Order No. R8-2015-0004, NPDES No. CAG998001, was adopted by the California\nL1246@P37: Regional Water Quality Control Board, Santa Ana Region (Regional Water Board) on June\nL1247@P37: 17, 2015 and became effective July 1, 2015. Order No. R8-2015-0004 is a general permit\nL1248@P37: for discharges to surface waters of various types of wastes that pose an insignificant threat\nL1249@P37: to water quality. It facilitated the processing of permit applications for many projects that\nL1250@P37: would otherwise need to be covered under individual waste discharge requirements.\nL1251@P37: Order No. R8-2015-0004 will expire on July 1, 2020. To date, 69 Dischargers have been\nL1252@P37: authorized to discharge wastewater under Order No. R8-2015-0004; of these, 44 are still\nL1253@P37: active. It is anticipated that many of these existing Dischargers will be submitting renewal\nL1254@P37: applications for continued discharges. The demand for permit issuance will far exceed the\nL1255@P37: available staff resources to develop and bring individual tentative waste discharge\nL1256@P37: requirements to the Board for adoption. These circumstances necessitate the renewal of\nL1257@P37: this general Order.\nL1258@P37: II. DISCHARGE INFORMATION\nL1259@P37: This Order regulates discharges of wastes that pose an insignificant (de minimis) threat to\nL1260@P37: water quality. The types of discharges regulated by this Order are essentially the same as\nL1261@P37: those regulated by Order No. R8-20015-0004, with the exception of certain discharges from\nL1262@P37: water purveyors. The State Water Resources Control Board (State Water Board) has\nL1263@P37: recently adopted a general order that addresses those discharges (Order No. WQ 2014-\nL1264@P37: 0194-DWQ). Therefore, discharges addressed through the State Water Board Order are\nL1265@P37-38: specifically excluded from this Order.Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL1266@P38: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL1267@P38: Attachment F – Fact Sheet Page F-3 of F-15\nL1268@P38: The types of wastewater discharges regulated under this Order include the following:\nL1269@P38: a. Construction dewatering wastes1;\nL1270@P38: b. Wastes associated with well installation, development, test pumping and purging2;\nL1271@P38: c. Aquifer testing wastes;\nL1272@P38: d. Dewatering wastes from subterranean seepage3;\nL1273@P38: e. Discharges resulting from hydrostatic testing of vessels, pipelines, tanks, etc.2;\nL1274@P38: f. Discharges resulting from the maintenance of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL1275@P38: reservoirs, etc.2;\nL1276@P38: g. Discharges resulting from the disinfection of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL1277@P38: reservoirs, etc.;\nL1278@P38: h. Discharges from potable water supply systems resulting from initial system start-up,\nL1279@P38: routine start-up, sampling of influent flow, system failures, pressure releases,etc.2;\nL1280@P38: i. Discharges from fire hydrant testing or flushing2;\nL1281@P38: j. Air conditioning condensate;\nL1282@P38: k. Swimming pool discharge;\nL1283@P38: l. Discharges resulting from diverted stream flows;\nL1284@P38: m. Decanted filter backwash wastewater and/or sludge dewatering filtrate waterfrom\nL1285@P38: water treatment facilities; and\nL1286@P38: n. Other similar types of wastes as determined by the Regional Water Board Executive\nL1287@P38: Officer, which pose a de minimis threat to water quality yet must be regulated under\nL1288@P38: waste discharge requirements.\nL1646@P47: provided in Attachment E. However, the number of constituents to be monitored and the\nL1647@P47: monitoring and reporting frequency may be modified on a case-by-case basis, based on\nL1648@P47: the nature of the discharge being authorized. Revision of each individual monitoring and\nL1649@P47: reporting program by the Executive Officer may be necessary to confirm that the\nL1650@P47: Discharger is in compliance with the requirements and provisions contained in this Order.\nL1651@P47: Revisions may be made by the Executive Officer at any time during the term of this Order,\nL1652@P47: and may include a reduction or an increase in the number of constituents to be monitored,\nL1653@P47: the frequency of monitoring, the number and size of samples collected, and the frequency\nL1654@P47: for report submittal.\nL1655@P47: A. Influent Monitoring – Not Applicable\nL1656@P47: B. Effluent Monitoring\nL1657@P47: Dischargers are required to conduct monitoring of the permitted discharges in order to\nL1658@P47: evaluate compliance with permit conditions and to allow ongoing characterization of\nL1659@P47: discharges to determine potential adverse impacts and to determine continued suitability\nL1660@P47: for coverage under the General Order.\nL1661@P47: In addition to discharge rate, effluent will typically be required to be monitored for pH, total\nL1662@P47: suspended solids, total petroleum hydrocarbons, sulfides and/or chlorine residual\nL1663@P47: depending on the nature of the discharge.\nL1664@P47-48: C. Whole Effluent Toxicity Testing Requirements – Not ApplicableOrder No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL1665@P48: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL1666@P48: Attachment F – Fact Sheet Page F-13 of F-15\nL1667@P48: D. Receiving Water Monitoring\nL1668@P48: Many of the effluent limitations in this Order are not applicable if the effluent does not\nL1669@P48: reach a flowing stream. Whenever there is a discharge and the Discharger asserts that\nL1670@P48: the discharge percolated before it reached a stream with aquatic life, the Discharger is\nL1671@P48: required to record in a permanent log the following information: (a) the date(s), time(s),\nL1672@P48: and duration(s) of the discharge; (b) a description of the location where the discharge(s)\nL1673@P48: percolated into the ground, (c) the climatic condition in the area during the discharge\nL1674@P48: and (d) the name of the individual(s) who performed the observation.\nL1675@P48: For discharges that do reach a stream, the Discharger is required to make visual\nL1676@P48: observations of the receiving water (only when discharge is occurring) on a weekly\nL1677@P48: basis for any visible oil sheen or coloration of the receiving water. The findings of these\nL1678@P48: observations are required to be recorded in a permanent log.\nL1679@P48: E. Reporting Requirements\nL1680@P48: Dischargers typically will be required to submit all of the data collected during the\nL1681@P48: previous month by the 30th day of the following month.\nL1682@P48: VIII.PROVISIONS\nL1683@P48: A. Standard Provisions\nL1684@P48: Standard Provisions, which apply to all NPDES permits in accordance with 40 CFR\nL1685@P48: 122.41, and additional conditions applicable to specified categories of permits in\nL1686@P48: accordance with 40 CFR 122.42, are provided in Attachment D.\nL1687@P48: 40 CFR 122.41(a)(1) and (b) through (n) establish conditions that apply to all State\u0002issued NPDES permits. These conditions must be incorporated into the permits either\nL1688@P48: expressly or by reference. If incorporated by reference, a specific citation to the\nL1689@P48: regulations must be included in the Order. 40 CFR 123.25(a)(12) allows the state to\nL1690@P48: omit or modify conditions to impose more stringent requirements. In accordance with 40\nL1691@P48: CFR 123.25, this Order omits federal conditions that address enforcement authority\nL1692@P48: specified in 40 CFR 122.41(j)(5) and (k)(2) because the enforcement authority underthe\nL1693@P48: California Water Code is more stringent. In lieu of these conditions, this Order\nL1694@P48: incorporates by reference Water Code section 13387(e).\nL1695@P48: B. Special Provisions\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nR8-2020-0006 Regional De Minimis Permit (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2020/R8-2020-0006.pdf)\nciteturn1082view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view2\",\"pattern\":\"expiration\"}); Total lines: 1765\nL726@P21-22: 122.41(n)(3)(ii));Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL727@P22: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL728@P22: Attachment D – Federal Standard Provisions Page 3 of D-8\nL729@P22: c. The Discharger submitted notice of the upset as required in StandardProvisions\nL730@P22: – Reporting V.E.2.b below (24-hour notice) (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(n)(3)(iii)); and\nL731@P22: d. The Discharger complied with any remedial measures requiredunder\nL732@P22: Standard Provisions – Permit Compliance I.C above. (40 C.F.R. §\nL733@P22: 122.41(n)(3)(iv))\nL734@P22: 3. Burden of proof. In any enforcement proceeding, the Discharger seeking to\nL735@P22: establish the occurrence of an upset has the burden of proof. (40 C.F.R. §\nL736@P22: 122.41(n)(4))\nL737@P22: II. STANDARD PROVISIONS – PERMIT ACTION\nL738@P22: A. General\nL739@P22: This Order may be modified, revoked and reissued, or terminated for cause. The filing\nL740@P22: of a request by the Discharger for modification, revocation and reissuance, or\nL741@P22: termination, or a notification of planned changes or anticipated noncompliance does not\nL742@P22: stay any Order condition. (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(f).)\nL743@P22: B. Duty to Reapply\nL744@P22: If the Discharger wishes to continue an activity regulated by this Order after the\nL745@P22: expiration date of this Order, the Discharger must apply for and obtain a new permit.\nL746@P22: (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(b).)\nL747@P22: C. Transfers\nL748@P22: This Order is not transferable to any person except after notice to the Regional Water\nL749@P22: Board. The Regional Water Board may require modification or revocation and\nL750@P22: reissuance of the Order to change the name of the Discharger and incorporate such\nL751@P22: other requirements as may be necessary under the CWA and the Water Code. (40\nL752@P22: C.F.R. § 122.41(l)(3); § 122.61.)\nL753@P22: III. STANDARD PROVISIONS – MONITORING\nL754@P22: A. Samples and measurements taken for the purpose of monitoring shall be representative\nL755@P22: of the monitored activity. (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(1).)\nL756@P22: B. Monitoring results must be conducted according to test procedures under Part 136 or, in\nL757@P22: the case of sludge use or disposal, approved under Part 136 unless otherwise specified\nL758@P22: in Part 503 unless other test procedures have been specified in this Order. (40 C.F.R. §\nL759@P22-23: 122.41(j)(4); § 122.44(i)(1)(iv).)Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL760@P23: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL761@P23: Attachment D – Federal Standard Provisions Page 4 of D-8\nL762@P23: IV. STANDARD PROVISIONS – RECORDS\nL763@P23: A. The Discharger shall retain records of all monitoring information, including all calibration\nL764@P23: and maintenance records and all original strip chart recordings for continuous\nL765@P23: monitoring instrumentation, copies of all reports required by this Order, and records of\nL766@P23: all data used to complete the application for this Order, for a period of at least three (3)\nL767@P23: years from the date of the sample, measurement, report or application. This period may\nL768@P23: be extended by request of the Regional Water Board Executive Officer at any time. (40\nL769@P23: C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(2).)\nL770@P23: B. Records of monitoring information shall include:\nL771@P23: 1. The date, exact place, and time of sampling or measurements (40 C.F.R. §\nL772@P23: 122.41(j)(3)(i));\nL773@P23: 2. The individual(s) who performed the sampling or measurements (40 C.F.R. §\nL774@P23: 122.41(j)(3)(ii));\nL775@P23: 3. The date(s) analyses were performed (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(3)(iii));\nL776@P23: 4. The individual(s) who performed the analyses (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(3)(iv));\nL777@P23: 5. The analytical techniques or methods used (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(3)(v)); and\nL778@P23: 6. The results of such analyses. (40 C.F.R. § 122.41(j)(3)(vi).)\nL779@P23: C. Claims of confidentiality for the following information will be denied (40 C.F.R. §\nL780@P23: 122.7(b)):\nL781@P23: 1. The name and address of any permit applicant or Discharger (40 C.F.R. §\nL782@P23: 122.7(b)(1)); and\nL783@P23: 2. Permit applications and attachments, permits and effluent data. (40 C.F.R. §\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nR8-2020-0006 Regional De Minimis Permit (https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/santaana/board_decisions/adopted_orders/orders/2020/R8-2020-0006.pdf)\nciteturn1082view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1077view2\",\"pattern\":\"pool\"}); Total lines: 1765\nL62@P2: I. DISCHARGE INFORMATION\nL63@P2: 1. The types of wastewater discharges regulated under this Order include the following:\nL64@P2: a. Construction dewatering wastes;1\nL65@P2: b. Wastes associated with well installation, development, test pumping and purging 2;\nL66@P2: c. Aquifer testing wastes;\nL67@P2: d. Dewatering wastes from subterranean seepage3(except for discharges from utility\nL68@P2: vaults);\nL69@P2: e. Discharges resulting from hydrostatic testing of vessels, pipelines, tanks, etc.2;\nL70@P2: f. Discharges resulting from the maintenance of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL71@P2: reservoirs, etc.;2\nL72@P2: g. Discharges resulting from the disinfection of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL73@P2: reservoirs, etc.2;\nL74@P2: h. Discharges from potable water supply systems resulting from initial system startup,\nL75@P2: routine startup, sampling of influent flow, system failures, pressure releases,etc.2;\nL76@P2: i. Discharges from fire hydrant testing or flushing2;\nL77@P2: j. Air conditioning condensate;\nL78@P2: k. Swimming pool discharge;\nL79@P2: l. Discharges resulting from diverted stream flows;\nL80@P2: m. Decanted filter backwash wastewater and/or sludge dewatering filtrate waterfrom\nL81@P2: water treatment facilities; and\nL82@P2: n. Other similar types of wastes as determined by the Regional Water BoardExecutive\nL83@P2: Officer, which pose a de minimis threat to water quality yet must be regulated under\nL84@P2: waste discharge requirements.\nL85@P2: 2. This Order regulates proposed groundwater related discharges and/or de minimis\nL86@P2: discharges within the San Diego Creek/Newport Bay Watershed that do not contain\nL87@P2: nutrients, selenium, and other pollutants of TMDL concern at levels that pose a threat to\nL88@P2: water quality.\nL89@P2: 3. The following discharges are excluded from regulation under this Order:\nL90@P2: a. Wastewater with pollutants of concern other than those for which effluentlimitations\nL91@P2: are specified in this Order.\nL92@P2: 1 Except for storm water discharges regulated under a statewide general constructionstorm\nL93@P2: water permit or a Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System permit.\nL94@P2: 2 Except for wastes discharged from water purveyors regulated under statewidegeneral\nL95@P2: waste discharge requirements, such as Order WQ 2014-0194-DWQ, NPDES No.\nL96@P2: CAG140001.\nL97@P2: 3 Except for waste discharges from utility vaults regulated under statewide general waste\nL98@P2: discharge requirements, such as Order No. 2014-0174-DWQ, NPDES No.CAG990002.\nL99@P2-3: De Minimis OrderOrder No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES No. CAG998001\nL100@P3: General WDRs for Insignificant Threat Discharges to Surface Waters Page 4 of 12\nL101@P3: b. Wastewater discharges from hydro-testing of contaminated pipes, vessels or tanks.\nL102@P3: c. Wastewater discharges from draining of decorative ponds, golf course lakes, etc.,\nL103@P3: unless full characterization of the wastewater for the presence of pesticides,\nL1265@P37-38: specifically excluded from this Order.Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL1266@P38: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL1267@P38: Attachment F – Fact Sheet Page F-3 of F-15\nL1268@P38: The types of wastewater discharges regulated under this Order include the following:\nL1269@P38: a. Construction dewatering wastes1;\nL1270@P38: b. Wastes associated with well installation, development, test pumping and purging2;\nL1271@P38: c. Aquifer testing wastes;\nL1272@P38: d. Dewatering wastes from subterranean seepage3;\nL1273@P38: e. Discharges resulting from hydrostatic testing of vessels, pipelines, tanks, etc.2;\nL1274@P38: f. Discharges resulting from the maintenance of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL1275@P38: reservoirs, etc.2;\nL1276@P38: g. Discharges resulting from the disinfection of potable water supply pipelines,tanks,\nL1277@P38: reservoirs, etc.;\nL1278@P38: h. Discharges from potable water supply systems resulting from initial system start-up,\nL1279@P38: routine start-up, sampling of influent flow, system failures, pressure releases,etc.2;\nL1280@P38: i. Discharges from fire hydrant testing or flushing2;\nL1281@P38: j. Air conditioning condensate;\nL1282@P38: k. Swimming pool discharge;\nL1283@P38: l. Discharges resulting from diverted stream flows;\nL1284@P38: m. Decanted filter backwash wastewater and/or sludge dewatering filtrate waterfrom\nL1285@P38: water treatment facilities; and\nL1286@P38: n. Other similar types of wastes as determined by the Regional Water Board Executive\nL1287@P38: Officer, which pose a de minimis threat to water quality yet must be regulated under\nL1288@P38: waste discharge requirements.\nL1289@P38: This Order regulates proposed groundwater related discharges and/or de minimis\nL1290@P38: discharges within the San Diego Creek/Newport Bay Watershed that do not contain\nL1291@P38: nutrients, selenium, and other pollutants of TMDL concern at levels that pose a threat to\nL1292@P38: water quality. However, discharges containing these pollutants at levels of concern must be\nL1293@P38: regulated by a separate general order, such as Order No. R8-2019-0061, NPDES No.\nL1294@P38: CAG918002 or individual waste discharge requirements.\nL1295@P38: Wastewater discharges specifically excluded from this Orderinclude:\nL1296@P38: a. Wastewater with pollutants of concern other than those for which effluentlimitations\nL1297@P38: are specified in this Order,\nL1298@P38: b. Wastewater discharges from hydro-testing of contaminated pipes, tanks, orvessels,\nL1299@P38: and,\nL1300@P38: c. Wastewater discharges from draining of decorative ponds, golf course lakes, etc.,\nL1301@P38: unless a full characterization of the wastewater for the presence of pesticides,\nL1302@P38: insecticides, biocides and/or other chemicals that may have been applied tothe\nL1303@P38: 1 Except for storm water discharges regulated under a statewide general constructionstorm\nL1304@P38: water permit or a Municipal Separate Storm Sewer System permit.\nL1305@P38: 2 Except for wastes discharged from water purveyors regulated under statewidegeneral\nL1306@P38: waste discharge requirements, such as Order WQ 2014-0194-DWQ, NPDES No.\nL1307@P38: CAG140001.\nL1308@P38: 3 Except for discharges from utility vaults regulated under statewide general waste discharge\nL1309@P38-39: requirements, such as Order No. 2014-0174-DWQ, NPDES No. CAG990002.Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL1310@P39: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL1311@P39: Attachment F – Fact Sheet Page F-4 of F-15\nL1312@P39: water is provided which shows that there are no pollutants present at levels of\nL1313@P39: concern.\nL1314@P39: The most common treatment required for de minimis discharges is settling and/or\nL1315@P39: dechlorination. Settling is used for those discharges with high settleable solids\nL1316@P39: concentration. Discharges with residual chlorine, such as wastewater from hydro-testing of\nL1317@P39: pipes and storage tanks, swimming pool drainage, and development and purging of wells,\nL1318@P39: must be dechlorinated, unless the concentration is depleted by natural processes prior to\nL1319@P39: mixing with the receiving water. If dechlorination is not accomplished naturally, the most\nL1320@P39: common method of dechlorination is with the use of chemicals.\nL1321@P39: Those discharges with high concentrations of total dissolved solids, total inorganic nitrogen,\nL1322@P39: selenium, phosphorous, and/or perchlorate may require advanced forms of treatment and\nL1323@P39: may need to be covered under individual permits. Unless otherwise determined based on\nL1324@P39: pollutant and/or site-specific circumstances, where wastewater discharges contain pollutant\nL1325@P39: concentrations lower than established objectives, EPA priority pollutant water quality\nL1326@P39: criteria, and/or maximum contaminant levels(MCLs) for drinking water, wastewater may be\nL1327@P39: discharged without treatment as they pose no reasonable potential to affect the water\nL1328@P39: quality or beneficial uses of receiving waters.\nL1329@P39: This Order authorizes permitted discharges to inland surface waters, estuarine, and ocean\nL1330@P39: waters within the Santa Ana Region. In some cases, the de minimis discharges addressed\nL1331@P39: by this Order are to storm drains or creeks that are typically dry in the summer. In these\nL1332@P39: cases, the wastewater discharges percolate into the underlying groundwater management\nL1333@P39: zones without reaching flowing surface water. During wet seasons, wastewater discharges\nL1334@P39: are most oftentimes into flowing surface water.\nL1335@P39: III. APPLICATION FOR COVERAGE UNDER THE GENERAL ORDER\nL1336@P39: This Order requires each Discharger currently regulated under Order No. R8-2015-0004\nL1337@P39: who wishes to continue ongoing regulatory coverage under this Order to submit the first\nL1338@P39: and last page of the Notice of Intent (NOI) form (Attachment B of this Order) by, August 1,\nL1339@P39: 2020. Dischargers who do not submit the updated NOI form by that date are no longer\nL1340@P39: authorized to discharge wastewater, unless the discharges are authorized pursuant toother\nL1341@P39: waste discharge requirements adopted by the Regional Water Board or State Water\nL1342@P39: Resources Control Board.\nL1343@P39: This Order requires each new Discharger4 to submit to the Executive Officer a complete\nL1344@P39: Notice of Intent for the proposed discharge at least 45 days before the start of a new\nL1345@P39: discharge. In addition to the basic information on the first page of the NOI form, new\nL1346@P39: Dischargers must include a complete characterization of the proposed discharge, which\nL1347@P39: includes, but is not limited to:\nL1348@P39: 4\nL1349@P39: “New discharger” refers to those proposing to discharge wastewater under Order No. R8-\nL1350@P39: 2020-0006 and not currently covered under Order No. R8-2015-0004 and those\nL1351@P39: Dischargers who were covered under Order No R8-2015-0004 and failed to submit an\nL1352@P39-40: updated NOI by August 1, 2020.Order No. R8-2020-0006, NPDES NO. CAG998001\nL1353@P40: General De Minimis Waste Discharge Requirements for Discharges to Surface Waters\nL1354@P40: Attachment F – Fact Sheet Page F-5 of F-15\nL1355@P40: a. A paragraph or more describing the actual project(s), and the types of wastewater\nL1356@P40: that are proposed to be discharged from the project(s) i.e. construction dewatering,\n"
